   "subject": "container model with DEB model",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Hi Mike,</div><div><br></div><div>I&#39;ve just started playing for the first time with the container model in ectotherm(), which has been cool for predicting e.g. changes in water levels over time. I&#39;m now trying to explore ectothermic development with the container model on (ultimately for frogs, but for now just to understand what&#39;s going on).</div><div><br></div><div>I&#39;m finding some seemingly strange behavior to DEB outputs when the container model is running. For example, attached is a script that pulls entirely from the <a href=\"https://48b294cf.streaklinks.com/CIiRi51DA-Ptu5Yh0APq42Nc/https%3A%2F%2Fgithub.com%2Fmrke%2FNicheMapR%2Fblob%2Fmaster%2Fvignettes%2Fectotherm-model-tutorial.Rmd\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://48b294cf.streaklinks.com/CIiRi51DA-Ptu5Yh0APq42Nc/https%253A%252F%252Fgithub.com%252Fmrke%252FNicheMapR%252Fblob%252Fmaster%252Fvignettes%252Fectotherm-model-tutorial.Rmd&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1793239760512000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3S5bEDMDy4vianTcsgLSHY\">DEB model vignette</a> (and as such draws from <a href=\"https://48b294cf.streaklinks.com/CIiRi51o6AfN72NnTgFCGcl-/https%3A%2F%2Fwww.bio.vu.nl%2Fthb%2Fdeb%2Fdeblab%2Fadd_my_pet%2FAmPdata%2FAmPdata.zip\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://48b294cf.streaklinks.com/CIiRi51o6AfN72NnTgFCGcl-/https%253A%252F%252Fwww.bio.vu.nl%252Fthb%252Fdeb%252Fdeblab%252Fadd_my_pet%252FAmPdata%252FAmPdata.zip&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1793239760512000&amp;usg=AOvVaw00V5NJZs5mKsrorjae9O1o\">AddMyPet allstat.rda</a>), but at the bottom I run ectotherm() twice: once with the container model off, and again with it on. It seems the predicted wetmass is constant at 50 g (even though Ww_g is left at default of 40, and all initial conditions for DEB left as-is), and the organism never develops beyond the first stage. The content from the DEB vignette wasn&#39;t meant to use the container model, but for this example I thought better to stick with content from the DEB vignette than to tweak further.<br></div><div><br></div><div>Any guesses why I&#39;m finding this? I dug through the archives of the Google Group, and looked back at the papers, and although there&#39;s some useful content on using the container model for predicting water temperatures (eg <a href=\"https://48b294cf.streaklinks.com/CIiRi5xs7CH2buYIOgpfjQ76/https%3A%2F%2Fgroups.google.com%2Fg%2Fnichemapr%2Fc%2Fl_QOFUFRNDo%2Fm%2F6Xv9lzmFAgAJ\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://48b294cf.streaklinks.com/CIiRi5xs7CH2buYIOgpfjQ76/https%253A%252F%252Fgroups.google.com%252Fg%252Fnichemapr%252Fc%252Fl_QOFUFRNDo%252Fm%252F6Xv9lzmFAgAJ&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1793239760512000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3JuPBU7XX3ZlhAaVyIpHZt\">here</a> on Google Group), I haven&#39;t found too much (yet) to help guide me on how the container model influences the DEB outputs. Closest papers seem to be <a href=\"https://48b294cf.streaklinks.com/CIiRi512dim6ghoLLwFzS-dU/https%3A%2F%2Fbesjournals.onlinelibrary.wiley.com%2Fdoi%2Ffull%2F10.1111%2Fj.1365-2435.2008.01538.x\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://48b294cf.streaklinks.com/CIiRi512dim6ghoLLwFzS-dU/https%253A%252F%252Fbesjournals.onlinelibrary.wiley.com%252Fdoi%252Ffull%252F10.1111%252Fj.1365-2435.2008.01538.x&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1793239760512000&amp;usg=AOvVaw05nTc1qtYQGYwHVncg3kwK\">Kearney et al. 2009 Functional Ecology</a>, and <a href=\"https://48b294cf.streaklinks.com/CIiRi51i4l6cTsrPCAmlkh60/https%3A%2F%2Fonlinelibrary.wiley.com%2Fdoi%2Ffull%2F10.1111%2Fgcb.14673%3Fcasa_token%3DhMXVBmA4CxAAAAAA%253AxDTGXgUeKBuE61-fJg3OCwd--Ul0XXPYJXvArTV4QpGDFO8mG5gPDaR2wxI654AA2O0W3wBSAufn-1fy\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://48b294cf.streaklinks.com/CIiRi51i4l6cTsrPCAmlkh60/https%253A%252F%252Fonlinelibrary.wiley.com%252Fdoi%252Ffull%252F10.1111%252Fgcb.14673%253Fcasa_token%253DhMXVBmA4CxAAAAAA%25253AxDTGXgUeKBuE61-fJg3OCwd--Ul0XXPYJXvArTV4QpGDFO8mG5gPDaR2wxI654AA2O0W3wBSAufn-1fy&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1793239760512000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1ZOH-0u4u3iRNVhaqV-fkb\">Urtzi&#39;s 2019 GCB paper</a> is relevant although I don&#39;t think he ran the container model.</div><div><br></div><div>Thanks!</div><div>Dave</div><div><br></div><span class=\"gmail_signature_prefix\">-- </span><br><div dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gmail_signature\" data-smartmail=\"gmail_signature\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\" style=\"font-size:small\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><font size=\"4\" color=\"#666666\" face=\"times new roman, serif\">David Klinges, PhD</font></div><div><img width=\"200\" height=\"75\" src=\"https://ci3.googleusercontent.com/mail-sig/AIorK4xY-XSJuf3_zCBzgEXqeW1GOzgHTiSkQRWnzpxOYB5iBxRI1fnIcrYjfvIVKZyFNUZdzsmZ8qLFeQZM\"><br></div><div><a href=\"https://natureinparadise.github.io/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://natureinparadise.github.io/&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1793239760512000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0GNncoK4yz5dvbIFsvwSEw\"><i>https://natureinparadise.github.io/</i></a></div></div><div><i><a href=\"https://48b294cf.streaklinks.com/CIiRi55UlBavAW0nFAKfRwXK/https%3A%2F%2Fscholar.google.com%2Fcitations%3Fuser%3Di9pmQasAAAAJ%26hl%3Den\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://48b294cf.streaklinks.com/CIiRi55UlBavAW0nFAKfRwXK/https%253A%252F%252Fsch