     "<div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div dir=\"auto\" class=\"gmail_attr\">On Sunday, September 29, 2024 at 6:40:42 PM UTC-4 NicheMapR wrote:<br></div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><div>Hi Dave,</div><div>I looked into this and it doesn&#39;t seem to be functioning properly. I wrote a fair bit of code for simulating semi-aquatic and aquatic organisms with the DEB model in the context of water availability but haven&#39;t finalised or published it so it&#39;s not really ready for general use. I&#39;ll try and get it going properly sometime in the next few months.</div><div>All the best,<br>Mike<br></div><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div dir=\"auto\" class=\"gmail_attr\">On Friday, September 27, 2024 at 10:11:21 AM UTC+10 <a rel=\"nofollow\">dkli...@gmail.com</a> wrote:<br></div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Hi Mike,</div><div><br></div><div>I&#39;ve just started playing for the first time with the container model in ectotherm(), which has been cool for predicting e.g. changes in water levels over time. I&#39;m now trying to explore ectothermic development with the container model on (ultimately for frogs, but for now just to understand what&#39;s going on).</div><div><br></div><div>I&#39;m finding some seemingly strange behavior to DEB outputs when the container model is running. For example, attached is a script that pulls entirely from the <a href=\"https://48b294cf.streaklinks.com/CIiRi51DA-Ptu5Yh0APq42Nc/https%3A%2F%2Fgithub.com%2Fmrke%2FNicheMapR%2Fblob%2Fmaster%2Fvignettes%2Fectotherm-model-tutorial.Rmd\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://48b294cf.streaklinks.com/CIiRi51DA-Ptu5Yh0APq42Nc/https%253A%252F%252Fgithub.com%252Fmrke%252FNicheMapR%252Fblob%252Fmaster%252Fvignettes%252Fectotherm-model-tutorial.Rmd&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1793239760515000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2EJOvINBLWVi3T4O4uoHt0\">DEB model vignette</a> (and as such draws from <a href=\"https://48b294cf.streaklinks.com/CIiRi51o6AfN72NnTgFCGcl-/https%3A%2F%2Fwww.bio.vu.nl%2Fthb%2Fdeb%2Fdeblab%2Fadd_my_pet%2FAmPdata%2FAmPdata.zip\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://48b294cf.streaklinks.com/CIiRi51o6AfN72NnTgFCGcl-/https%253A%252F%252Fwww.bio.vu.nl%252Fthb%252Fdeb%252Fdeblab%252Fadd_my_pet%252FAmPdata%252FAmPdata.zip&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1793239760515000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1ebin_BJGpI9S8T-H8q9xW\">AddMyPet allstat.rda</a>), but at the bottom I run ectotherm() twice: once with the container model off, and again with it on. It seems the predicted wetmass is constant at 50 g (even though Ww_g is left at default of 40, and all initial conditions for DEB left as-is), and the organism never develops beyond the first stage. The content from the DEB vignette wasn&#39;t meant to use the container model, but for this example I thought better to stick with content from the DEB vignette than to tweak further.<br></div><div><br></div><div>Any guesses why I&#39;m finding this? I dug through the archives of the Google Group, and looked back at the papers, and although there&#39;s some useful content on using the container model for predicting water temperatures (eg <a href=\"https://48b294cf.streaklinks.com/CIiRi5xs7CH2buYIOgpfjQ76/https%3A%2F%2Fgroups.google.com%2Fg%2Fnichemapr%2Fc%2Fl_QOFUFRNDo%2Fm%2F6Xv9lzmFAgAJ\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://48b294cf.streaklinks.com/CIiRi5xs7CH2buYIOgpfjQ76/https%253A%252F%252Fgroups.google.com%252Fg%252Fnichemapr%252Fc%252Fl_QOFUFRNDo%252Fm%252F6Xv9lzmFAgAJ&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1793239760515000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3_N19pXxFGgWpcjlTP90Tw\">here</a> on Google Group), I haven&#39;t found too much (yet) to help guide me on how the container model influences the DEB outputs. Closest papers seem to be <a href=\"https://48b294cf.streaklinks.com/CIiRi512dim6ghoLLwFzS-dU/https%3A%2F%2Fbesjournals.onlinelibrary.wiley.com%2Fdoi%2Ffull%2F10.1111%2Fj.1365-2435.2008.01538.x\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://48b294cf.streaklinks.com/CIiRi512dim6ghoLLwFzS-dU/https%253A%252F%252Fbesjournals.onlinelibrary.wiley.com%252Fdoi%252Ffull%252F10.1111%252Fj.1365-2435.2008.01538.x&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1793239760515000&amp;usg=AOvVaw16ZjQ1fzaBYWUtL-7wAE0u\">Kearney et al. 2009 Functional Ecology</a>, and <a href=\"https://48b294cf.streaklinks.com/CIiRi51i4l6cTsrPCAmlkh60/https%3A%2F%2Fonlinelibrary.wiley.com%2Fdoi%2Ffull%2F10.1111%2Fgcb.14673%3Fcasa_token%3DhMXVBmA4CxAAAAAA%253AxDTGXgUeKBuE61-fJg3OCwd--Ul0XXPYJXvArTV4QpGDFO8mG5gPDaR2wxI654AA2O0W3wBSAufn-1fy\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://48b294cf.streaklinks.com/CIiRi51i4l6cTsrPCAmlkh60/https%253A%252F%252Fonlinelibrary.wiley.com%252Fdoi%252Ffull%252F10.1111%252Fgcb.14673%253Fcasa_token%253DhMXVBmA4CxAAAAAA%25253AxDTGXgUeKBuE61-fJg3OCwd--Ul0XXPYJXvArTV4QpGDFO8mG5gPDaR2wxI654AA2O0W3wBSAufn-1fy&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1793239760515000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0aMEmJjUv6bHkgE-8axszS\">Urtzi&#39;s 2019 GCB paper</a> is relevant although I don&#39;t think he ran the container model.</div><div><br></div><div>Thanks!</div><div>Dave</div><div><br></div><span class=\"gmail_signature_prefix\">-- </span><br><div dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gmail_signature\" data-smartmail=\"gmail_signature\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\" style=\"font-size:small\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><font size=\"4\" color=\"#666666\" face=\"times new roman, serif\">David Klinges, PhD</font></div><div><img width=\"200\" height=\"75\" src=\"https://ci3.googleusercontent.com/mail-sig/AIorK4xY-XSJuf3_zCBzgEXqeW1GOzgHTiSkQRWnzpxOYB5iBxRI1fnIcrYjfvIVKZyFNUZdzsmZ8qLFeQZM\"><br></div><div><a href=\"https://natureinparadise.github.io/\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://natureinparadise.github.io/&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1793239760515000&amp;usg=AOvVaw321gn_S13nDdySPBRS9cin\"><i>https://natureinparadise.github.io/</i></a></div></div><div><i><a href=\"https://48b294cf.streaklinks.com/CIiRi55UlBavAW0nFAKfRwXK/https%3A%2F%2Fscholar.google.com%2Fcitations%3Fuser%3Di9pmQasAAAAJ%26hl%3Den\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://48b294cf.streaklinks.com/CIiRi55UlBavAW0nFAKfRwXK/https%253A%252F%252Fscholar.google.com%252Fcitations%253Fuser%253Di9pmQasAAAAJ%2526hl%253Den&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1793239760515000&amp;usg=AOvVaw11RiGMkQQRh7OBLglMTxAR\"><font color=\"#0000ff\">Google Scholar Page</font></a></i></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div><div hspace=\"streak-pt-mark\" style=\"max-height:1px\"><img alt=\"\" style=\"width:0px;max-height:0px;overflow:hidden\" src=\"https://ci3.googleusercontent.com/proxy/5g-5qjgbVbe5C-ywuRFr5XR65QGMeN05eQqU2vcUIFZu0dC93g5DSmd2heMneKeGCDRExod3pNZnzP6hB3BhaOx7XSwO_9SCdVWnjqhziNvaKBY5hPp4MiKIZeKR1USe67oxZgWSI8QXpI75-FWTzoxwiceweX6R_DoiBbusC4LKkYs0w1cA32_tuwG7LipkTs-FHxAgI9o=s0-d-e1-ft#https://mailfoogae.appspot.com/t?sender=aZGtsaW5nZXM5QGdtYWlsLmNvbQ%3D%3D&amp;type=zerocontent&amp;guid=7c774161-30b8-4ef9-9454-38513a4725e4\"><font color=\"#ffffff\" size=\"1\">ᐧ</font></div>\r\n</blockquote></div></blockquote></div>"
